+	 * their tabulated pressure-drag curve at the drag-divergence Mach with the
+	 * value 0, and the fineness-ratio extrapolation above is multiplicative, so a
+	 * short example of these shapes is left with (essentially) zero subsonic
+	 * pressure drag at every fineness ratio. That is non-physical for a blunt
+	 * body: a stubby ellipsoid or Von Karman has a real, measurable subsonic
+	 * pressure drag. This applies a floor, as max(existing, floor) over the whole
+	 * subsonic range including the leading tabulated point, so the shape does not
+	 * sit at the floor and then jump at its drag-divergence Mach.
+	 * <p>
